module std.page_fold

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, Int, Bool, List, String }
import std.integer { PositiveInt }
import extdeps.uri { Uri }

// THREE CLAIMS, NOT ONE, AND CONFLATING THEM IS WHY THIS MODULE DID NOT EXIST FOR SO LONG.
//
// A listing read makes three separable claims and every one of them can be true while the others
// are false. (1) PAGE SIZE is how many items one request asks for -- a request parameter, bounded
// by the serving authority. (2) CURSOR SHAPE is how the next request is named -- an offset, an
// opaque token, or a link target, and they are different protocols rather than spellings of one.
// (3) CONTINUATION CLOSURE is whether the sequence of requests was consumed until the authority
// said there was no next -- a property of the WHOLE fold, which no single response carries.
//
// Two sessions read a `per_page` field and concluded pagination was modeled: they substituted
// claim (1) for claim (3). The substitution is easy precisely because claim (1) is the one that
// appears lexically in a request declaration while claim (3) appears nowhere unless a fold
// exists. So the three are declared here as three carriers, and no projection of one answers for
// another.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: the effect that fetches a page. This module is the pure driver --
// it decides, given what a page said, whether another request is owed, whether the fold is closed,
// or whether it must refuse. The transport that performs the request is a realization bound at the
// caller's boundary (DESIGN section 3: the dispatch that selects a realization is itself
// realization). The driver is total and executable against a recorded page sequence, which is what
// page_fold_run consumes.

// THE BOUND IS THE POINT, AND IT IS A CONSTRUCTOR RATHER THAN A CHECK.
//
// An external listing has no modeled length. A fold that follows continuations until the authority
// stops is an unbounded external read, and an unbounded external read is the absorbing fallback
// DESIGN section 5 forbids: a serving side that keeps answering `next` -- through a defect, a
// cursor that does not advance, or a corpus larger than anyone budgeted -- turns a read into a
// non-terminating one, and the honest arms are exhaustion or refusal, never a truncated result
// wearing the shape of a complete one.
//
// max_pages is PositiveInt rather than Int because a zero-page budget refuses every fold before it
// begins, which is a policy nobody means and which would report the same refusal as a genuine
// overrun. The single authority for positivity is std.integer, consumed rather than re-minted.
type PageBound {
  max_pages: PositiveInt
}

// PAGE SIZE, CLAIM ONE, AND ITS ADMISSION AGAINST A SERVING MAXIMUM.
//
// The serving maximum is a fact about the authority being read, so it is supplied by the extdeps
// module that cites it and is never defaulted here. The admission REFUSES an over-large request
// rather than clamping it: an authority that silently clamps (GitHub's REST API is documented to)
// answers a request that was never made, and the caller has no way to learn that the size it
// reasoned about is not the size it got. Refusing keeps the disagreement visible at the boundary
// where it is still cheap.
type PageSize {
  count: PositiveInt
}

type PageSizeAdmission
  = PageSizeAdmitted { size: PageSize }
  | PageSizeAboveAuthorityMaximum { requested: PageSize, maximum: PageSize }

fn admit_page_size(requested: PageSize, maximum: PageSize) -> PageSizeAdmission {
  if requested.count > maximum.count {
    PageSizeAboveAuthorityMaximum { requested: requested, maximum: maximum }
  } else {
    PageSizeAdmitted { size: requested }
  }
}

// CURSOR SHAPE, CLAIM TWO, AS A CLOSED COPRODUCT OF THE FORMS REAL AUTHORITIES DOCUMENT.
//
// It is closed rather than a String because the three forms have different advance laws and
// different failure modes, and a String erases which one is in play. An offset advances by
// arithmetic the CLIENT performs and is therefore the form that can loop forever if the server
// stops honouring it; an opaque token advances only by echoing what the server said and must never
// be interpreted; a link target is a whole request the server authored (RFC 8288 rel="next"), so
// the client composes no request at all.
//
// Adding a fourth form is a row here, and the exhaustive matches below fail to compile until every
// consumer has said what it means -- which is the point of closing it.
type PageCursor
  = OffsetPage { page: PositiveInt }
  | OpaqueToken { token: NonEmptyStr }
  | LinkNext { target: Uri }

fn page_cursor_shape_key(c: PageCursor) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match c {
    OffsetPage { page: p } => "offset_page" as NonEmptyStr
    OpaqueToken { token: t } => "opaque_token" as NonEmptyStr
    LinkNext { target: u } => "link_next" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

fn page_cursor_eq(a: PageCursor, b: PageCursor) -> Bool {
  match a {
    OffsetPage { page: pa } => match b {
      OffsetPage { page: pb } => pa == pb
      OpaqueToken { token: tb } => false
      LinkNext { target: ub } => false
    }
    OpaqueToken { token: ta } => match b {
      OffsetPage { page: pb } => false
      OpaqueToken { token: tb } => ta == tb
      LinkNext { target: ub } => false
    }
    LinkNext { target: ua } => match b {
      OffsetPage { page: pb } => false
      OpaqueToken { token: tb } => false
      LinkNext { target: ub } => ua.locator == ub.locator
    }
  }
}

// CONTINUATION CLOSURE, CLAIM THREE. What ONE page says about whether another is owed.
//
// ContinuationClosed is the authority's statement that the sequence ends here. It is not the same
// fact as "this page was short", and the difference is why a short-page heuristic is not offered:
// a page shorter than the requested size is evidence about the serving side's batching, not about
// exhaustion, and reading it as exhaustion silently truncates exactly the listings whose last full
// page happens to land on a boundary.
type PageContinuation
  = ContinuationClosed
  | ContinuationNext { cursor: PageCursor }

// THE SUBJECT IS WHAT MAKES A REFUSAL LOCATED. A refusal that says only "the page budget ran out"
// names no operation, so a consumer holding several folds cannot tell which listing is incomplete.
type PageFoldSubject {
  operation: NonEmptyStr
}

// EVERY REFUSAL ARM CARRIES ITS SUBJECT AND ITS POSITION, and none of them carries a partial
// result. That absence is the wall: DESIGN section 5 requires the failure arm to refuse rather
// than widen, and a refusal that also handed back the items read so far would let a caller use a
// truncated listing by reading one field and ignoring the other.
//
// The arms are separate because their remedies are separate. A budget overrun is a policy question
// -- raise the bound, or narrow the query. A non-advancing cursor is a defect on the serving side
// or in the cursor projection, and folding it into the budget arm would report a real infinite
// loop as a budget that was set too low, sending the operator to change the wrong number. A shape
// change mid-fold means two protocols answered one listing. A fold still open when the page
// sequence ran out is the caller's own truncation, and it is the arm that catches a transport
// that stopped early without saying so.
type PageFoldRefusal
  = PageBudgetExhausted {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      max_pages: PositiveInt
      pages_read: Int
      stopped_at: PageCursor
    }
  | CursorDidNotAdvance {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      page_index: Int
      cursor: PageCursor
    }
  | ContinuationShapeChanged {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      page_index: Int
      from_shape: NonEmptyStr
      to_shape: NonEmptyStr
    }
  | ContinuationNotObserved {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      pages_read: Int
      awaiting: PageCursor
    }
  | PageObservedAfterFoldEnded {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      pages_read: Int
    }

fn page_fold_refusal_subject(r: PageFoldRefusal) -> PageFoldSubject {
  match r {
    PageBudgetExhausted { subject: s, max_pages: m, pages_read: n, stopped_at: c } => s
    CursorDidNotAdvance { subject: s, page_index: i, cursor: c } => s
    ContinuationShapeChanged { subject: s, page_index: i, from_shape: f, to_shape: t } => s
    ContinuationNotObserved { subject: s, pages_read: n, awaiting: c } => s
    PageObservedAfterFoldEnded { subject: s, pages_read: n } => s
  }
}

fn page_fold_refusal_key(r: PageFoldRefusal) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r {
    PageBudgetExhausted { subject: s, max_pages: m, pages_read: n, stopped_at: c } => "page_budget_exhausted" as NonEmptyStr
    CursorDidNotAdvance { subject: s, page_index: i, cursor: c } => "cursor_did_not_advance" as NonEmptyStr
    ContinuationShapeChanged { subject: s, page_index: i, from_shape: f, to_shape: t } => "continuation_shape_changed" as NonEmptyStr
    ContinuationNotObserved { subject: s, pages_read: n, awaiting: c } => "continuation_not_observed" as NonEmptyStr
    PageObservedAfterFoldEnded { subject: s, pages_read: n } => "page_observed_after_fold_ended" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

// ONE PAGE AS THE DRIVER SEES IT: the items it carried, and what it said about a next one.
type PageObservation<I> {
  items: List<I>
  continuation: PageContinuation
}

type PageFoldStatus
  = FoldOpen { next: PageCursor }
  | FoldEnded
  | FoldRefused { refusal: PageFoldRefusal }

type PageFoldState<I> {
  subject: PageFoldSubject
  bound: PageBound
  pages_read: Int
  items: List<I>
  status: PageFoldStatus
}

fn page_fold_begin<I>(subject: PageFoldSubject, bound: PageBound, first: PageCursor) -> PageFoldState<I> {
  PageFoldState {
    subject: subject
    bound: bound
    pages_read: 0
    items: []
    status: FoldOpen { next: first }
  }
}

// WHAT THE DRIVER OWES BEFORE IT MAY OBSERVE ANOTHER PAGE. A caller drives the transport from this
// projection rather than from the state's fields, so "is another request owed" is answered in one
// place instead of once per transport.
type PageFoldDemand
  = DemandPage { cursor: PageCursor }
  | DemandNone

fn page_fold_demand<I>(state: PageFoldState<I>) -> PageFoldDemand {
  match state.status {
    FoldOpen { next: c } => DemandPage { cursor: c }
    FoldEnded => DemandNone
    FoldRefused { refusal: r } => DemandNone
  }
}

// THE ONE TRANSITION. Every arm below either ends the fold, refuses, or advances to a STRICTLY
// different cursor, so the state machine cannot revisit a cursor it has already demanded and the
// page count rises by exactly one per observation -- the descent that makes the bound a real bound
// rather than a hope.
//
// THE BOUND IS CHECKED WHEN A FURTHER PAGE IS DEMANDED, not when one is delivered, and the
// distinction is load-bearing. A listing that fits in exactly max_pages pages and then says
// ContinuationClosed must CLOSE, not refuse: checking on delivery would refuse the last page of
// every listing whose length happens to equal the budget, which is a false incompleteness report
// about a listing that was in fact read to exhaustion.
fn page_fold_observe<I>(state: PageFoldState<I>, page: PageObservation<I>) -> PageFoldState<I> {
  match state.status {
    FoldEnded => PageFoldState {
      subject: state.subject
      bound: state.bound
      pages_read: state.pages_read
      items: state.items
      status: FoldRefused {
        refusal: PageObservedAfterFoldEnded { subject: state.subject, pages_read: state.pages_read }
      }
    }
    FoldRefused { refusal: r } => state
    FoldOpen { next: current } =>
      let read = state.pages_read + 1
      let carried = concat(state.items, page.items)
      match page.continuation {
        ContinuationClosed => PageFoldState {
          subject: state.subject
          bound: state.bound
          pages_read: read
          items: carried
          status: FoldEnded
        }
        ContinuationNext { cursor: nxt } =>
          if page_cursor_eq(a: current, b: nxt) {
            PageFoldState {
              subject: state.subject
              bound: state.bound
              pages_read: read
              items: state.items
              status: FoldRefused {
                refusal: CursorDidNotAdvance { subject: state.subject, page_index: read, cursor: nxt }
              }
            }
          } else {
            if page_cursor_shape_key(c: current) != page_cursor_shape_key(c: nxt) {
              PageFoldState {
                subject: state.subject
                bound: state.bound
                pages_read: read
                items: state.items
                status: FoldRefused {
                  refusal: ContinuationShapeChanged {
                    subject: state.subject
                    page_index: read
                    from_shape: page_cursor_shape_key(c: current)
                    to_shape: page_cursor_shape_key(c: nxt)
                  }
                }
              }
            } else {
              if read >= state.bound.max_pages {
                PageFoldState {
                  subject: state.subject
                  bound: state.bound
                  pages_read: read
                  items: state.items
                  status: FoldRefused {
                    refusal: PageBudgetExhausted {
                      subject: state.subject
                      max_pages: state.bound.max_pages
                      pages_read: read
                      stopped_at: nxt
                    }
                  }
                }
              } else {
                PageFoldState {
                  subject: state.subject
                  bound: state.bound
                  pages_read: read
                  items: carried
                  status: FoldOpen { next: nxt }
                }
              }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE OUTCOME CARRIES THE ITEMS ONLY ON THE CLOSED ARM. This is the whole safety claim of the
// module stated as a constructor: there is no way to obtain a list of items from a fold that did
// not reach the authority's own end-of-listing, so a truncated listing is not a value a consumer
// can hold, let alone one it can mistake for a complete one.
type PageFoldOutcome<I>
  = PagesClosed {
      subject: PageFoldSubject
      pages_read: Int
      items: List<I>
    }
  | PagesRefused { refusal: PageFoldRefusal }

fn page_fold_finish<I>(state: PageFoldState<I>) -> PageFoldOutcome<I> {
  match state.status {
    FoldEnded => PagesClosed {
      subject: state.subject
      pages_read: state.pages_read
      items: state.items
    }
    FoldRefused { refusal: r } => PagesRefused { refusal: r }
    FoldOpen { next: c } => PagesRefused {
      refusal: ContinuationNotObserved {
        subject: state.subject
        pages_read: state.pages_read
        awaiting: c
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE DRIVER OVER A RECORDED PAGE SEQUENCE. It is a fold over the pages a transport actually
// delivered, so it executes -- under the interpreter, over a controlled fixture, or over a
// replayed live read -- rather than describing what an executing fold would do.
//
// A live transport calls page_fold_demand / page_fold_observe in its own loop instead; this
// projection exists because the two must not be two state machines, and the recorded form is the
// one a witness can drive.
fn page_fold_run<I>(
  subject: PageFoldSubject,
  bound: PageBound,
  first: PageCursor,
  pages: List<PageObservation<I>>
) -> PageFoldOutcome<I> {
  page_fold_finish(
    state: fold_list(
      xs: pages,
      empty: page_fold_begin(subject: subject, bound: bound, first: first),
      cons: fn(st, p) { page_fold_observe(state: st, page: p) }
    )
  )
}

// THE RESIDUE, NAMED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE DISCOVERED: nothing in this module performs a request.
// A .dag caller that must page a live listing today binds the transport at its own realization
// boundary and drives the two projections above, because the substrate has no construct for an
// effectful loop whose continuation is decided by the value the previous effect returned -- Loop
// is bounded and forward over a value the graph already carries, and a page sequence is not such a
// value until the reads have happened.
//
// THE CAPABILITY THAT WOULD CLOSE IT is an effect-driven bounded loop: a Loop whose iteration
// count is bounded by a declared budget and whose per-iteration input is the typed result of a
// declared effect, with the refusal on budget exhaustion raised by the loop rather than by the
// caller. Until that exists, page_fold_run over a recorded sequence is the executing form and the
// live form is a realization binding; this is a stated boundary, not a rung claim about live reads.
data page_fold_effect_loop_capability_trigger: String = "std.page_fold drives a page sequence purely and does not perform the reads. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: an effect-driven bounded Loop construct in the substrate -- iteration count bounded by a declared budget, per-iteration input the typed result of a declared effect, budget exhaustion refused by the loop itself. Naming a transport, a handler, or one paginated operation does NOT satisfy this trigger: those are one caller each, and the loss is that EVERY live listing read in the tree is driven by hand-bound realization rather than by a modeled construct."
